  "query_preview": "(This evening through Sunday)...\nIssued at 208 PM EDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nSurface analysis this afternoon shows strong high pressure \nstretching across the Great Lakes, Ontario to Quebec. This was \nresulting in a dry and cooler flow of air to Central Indiana. GOES19 \nshows mainly clear skies across the area and the surface trough and \nhumidity had pushed farther south to Alabama/MS/GA. Water Vapor \ncontinues to show strong ridging in place over the northern plains \nwith NW flow spilling SE to MI and Indiana. Water Vapor shows \nsubsidence in place over Indiana. Dew points across the area were \nmuch more pleasant, in the upper 50s and lower 60s.\n\nTonight...\n\nLittle overall change will be expected tonight. The high pressure to \nthe NE will sag southward, maintaining control of our weather. \nForecast soundings and time heights continue to show a dry column. \nThus a clear night will be expected along with cooler low \ntemperatures in the upper 50s.\n\nMonday through Tuesday...\n\nPleasant late summer weather is expected for the holiday and as many \nfolks return to work on Tuesday. Models shows the upper ridge axis \nto the west slowly advancing eastward toward Indiana. This steers \nany forcing dynamics well north of Central Indiana to Ontario, \nkeeping our area protected from any forcing. The surface high \npressure system to the northeast is expected to push to the eastern \nGreat Lakes on Monday, continuing the cool and dry easterly surface \nflow to Indiana. On Tuesday, that high will sag southeast, reaching \nthe middle Atlantic states. This will continue to keep the dry \nweather in place across Central Indiana, but southerly flow will \nreturn, allowing for warmer highs in the upper 80s. Forecast \nsoundings each day show a dry column with a sufficient mid level \ninversion to prevent CU development. Thus mostly sunny days and \nmostly clear nights will be expected.\n\nWednesday through Sunday...\n\nThe upper pattern is much more progressive late this week and into \nthe weekend compared to what we have experience the past week. A \nnearly steady Pacific, zonal flow is suggested to stream across the \nnorthern CONUS. A few embedded waves look to pass, mainly on \nWednesday night into Thursday, followed by another wave on Saturday \ninto Sunday.\n\nBest chances for rain will be on Wednesday Night in to Thursday as \nsurface cold front is suggested to accompany the upper forcing. \nAfter high pressure passes through the area on Thursday Night \nthrough Friday, more storm chances will arrive on Saturday and \nSunday as a less organized warm front arrives ahead of low pressure \ndeveloping over the plains. This will bring warmer high temperatures \nto the area for next weekend. Overall, look for above normal \ntemperatures as the quick nearly zonal upper flow aloft keeps any \ncool air intrusions at bay.",
